  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 11,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.378,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.122,
            0.409,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Prime factorisation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.145,
            0.885,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a number such as 56. It is composite, as we saw that it can be written as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "56 = 4 \\times 14.50"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , both 4 and 14 are factors of 56. Now take one of these, say 14. It is also composite and can be written as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 4 = 2 \\times 7 ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Therefore, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 6 = 4 \\times 2 \\times 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Now, 4 is composite and can be written as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4 = 2 \\times 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Therefore, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 6 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . All the factors appearing here, 2 and 7, are prime numbers. So, we cannot divide them further."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.282,
            0.884,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In conclusion, we have written 56 as a product of prime numbers. This is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "prime factorisation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of 56. The individual factors are called prime factors. For example, the prime factors of 56 are 2 and 7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.35,
            0.884,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Every number greater than 1 has a prime factorisation. The idea is the same: keep breaking the composite numbers into factors till only primes are left."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.418,
            0.884,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The number 1 does not have any prime factorisation. It is not divisible by any prime number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.463,
            0.884,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the prime factorisation of a prime number like 7? It is just 7 (we cannot break it down any further)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.509,
            0.541,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us see a few more examples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.532,
            0.885,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "By going through different ways of breaking down the number, we wrote 63 as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 \\times 3 \\times 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 \\times 7 \\times 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Are they different? Not really! The same prime numbers 3 and 7 occur in both cases. Further,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.6,
            0.434,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3 appears two times in both and 7 appears once."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.647,
            0.434,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here, you see four different ways get prime factorisation of 36. Observe that in all four cases, we get two 2s and two 3s."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.44,
            0.601,
            0.881,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.44,
                0.601,
                0.881,
                0.771
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.782,
            0.434,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Multiply back to see"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.805,
            0.504,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "that you get 36 in all four cases."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.828,
            0.885,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For any number, it is a remarkable fact that there is only one prime factorisation, except that the prime factors may come in different"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 15,
